                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_system:        `<strong>That's the right frame.</strong> The rail, the climate budget and the disclosure are one system: the rail changes your emissions and cost base, the EUR 2.4M is what your report discloses, and cheap offsets plus a thin disclosure is the greenwashing trigger. Now your three decisions can tell one story.`,
                    coach_r1_parallel:      `<strong>Three teams in three rooms is how the stories contradict each other.</strong> Payments picks a rail that raises emissions while sustainability writes that they fell. Double materiality means your operations and your disclosure are the same story — integrate now, not at year-end.`,
                    coach_r1_sequential:    `<strong>Sequencing the climate and disclosure work to 'later' is a trap.</strong> The covenant test and my Tuesday deadline don't wait for you to finish optimising basis points. Fix the P&L by all means — but the rail decision you make first is exactly what the report has to disclose.`,
                    coach_r1_reporting:     `<strong>Treating CSRD as a filing exercise is Common Error number five.</strong> If the rail and climate spend are made in one room and the report written in another, the numbers won't match — and that mismatch is the exposé.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp CFO diagnosis.</strong> You framed it as one system AND named the real exposure — a covenant breach plus a regulator look plus a P&L hit, all at once. That is the integrated lens that lets the three decisions reinforce rather than contradict each other.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>'Each problem is survivable on its own' is exactly what the journalist is hoping you believe.</strong> They compound: a weak disclosure feeds the greenwashing story, which trips the covenant, which reprices your capital. They are one exposure, not three.`,
                    coach_r1_lowintegration: `<strong>Low commitment to consistency is how the report and the operations drift apart.</strong> If you won't hold the three decisions to one narrative, the offset split and the rail's emissions will say different things — and I will find the gap.`,
                    coach_r2_hybrid:        `<strong>A phased hybrid is the modelled choice.</strong> Keeping cards while you migrate low-risk segments to A2A captures a real saving without betting the volume on an unfamiliar flow. Now make sure the adoption and fraud-ops investment matches the pace.`,
                    coach_r2_a2a:           `<strong>Full A2A can work — if you funded the adoption.</strong> The 0.35% rate is only real if users don't abandon the unfamiliar flow and the fraud desk can cope. You've moved hard; show me the adoption and fraud assumptions behind the saving.`,
                    coach_r2_a2aheadline:   `<strong>You moved everyone to instant-SEPA and banked EUR 9M on paper — with no adoption or fraud-ops money.</strong> Three months in, up to 40% hit an unfamiliar checkout, ~12% of volume churns to the incumbent, and your fraud desk is overwhelmed. How much of that EUR 9M did you actually keep?`,
                    coach_r2_nofraud:       `<strong>You're migrating volume with nothing behind it.</strong> Zero adoption nudges and no fraud-ops desk on a new A2A rail means churn and AML/SCA disputes you didn't price. The headline rate is not the rate you'll realise.`,
                    coach_r2_cards:         `<strong>You stayed on cards 'for safety' and left the swing on the table.</strong> That's an EUR 8–12M annual premium on a company with a ~EUR 5M EBITDA line. Where is the EUR 2.4M climate budget coming from if you just spent double your EBITDA on payment fees?`,
                    coach_r2_emisignore:    `<strong>Rail choice is also an emissions choice.</strong> Ignore it and your sustainability lead estimates the footprint blind at year-end — and it won't match the rail and cloud setup you actually picked. That mismatch is what double materiality exposes.`,
                    coach_r3_abate:         `<strong>Abatement-weighted spend is the credible play.</strong> Cutting real emissions in your cloud and payments stack is what investors and the regulator can verify — and it's the spend that survives my offset question. Slow and costly, but it's the one that holds up.`,
                    coach_r3_offsetonly:    `<strong>You bought 240,000 tonnes of offsets at EUR 10 and called it climate leadership.</strong> I found two of your projects already flagged as non-additional. Your impact investors are asking why a payments company isn't cutting its own cloud and transaction emissions. This is the greenwashing story writing itself — defend the spend.`,
                    coach_r3_mixed:         `<strong>A spread across the spectrum is defensible</strong> — but tilt it. The more weight on real abatement and a genuine customer product, and the less on cheap offsets, the harder it is for me to call it greenwashing.`,
                    coach_r3_ignorealign:   `<strong>Treating the climate budget as a standalone line breaks the story.</strong> If the spend doesn't target where the rail and cloud decision added emissions, your report claims one thing while your operations did another. Align it, or the double-materiality gap is the headline.`,
                    coach_r4_honest:        `<strong>An honest, assurable disclosure is the durable one.</strong> Reporting the offset-vs-abatement split transparently and committing only to evidenced targets is what survives assurance and scrutiny. It reads as less impressive today and far stronger when I check it.`,
                    coach_r4_glowing:       `<strong>You committed to net-zero by 2030 and 'industry-leading transparency'.</strong> Your assurer can't sign off the emissions baseline, and you've created a public target with no funded plan to hit it. Over-disclosure is a liability too — what can you actually evidence and assure?`,
                    coach_r4_minimal:       `<strong>Filing the bare minimum and hiding the offset split looks like you have something to hide.</strong> Minimal disclosure satisfies the letter of ESRS but tells investors and me that the climate story is thin. That silence is its own signal.`,
                    coach_r4_spin:          `<strong>Restating the mission and refusing to break out the offsets is spin, and I'll write it that way.</strong> 'Good for your wallet and the planet' is the claim under question — repeating it instead of evidencing it is exactly what an exposé needs.`,
                    coach_r4_silence:       `<strong>No comment hands me the narrative.</strong> You spent 80 minutes on basis points and forgot I have a Tuesday deadline and your investors have a covenant test this quarter. Silence reads as guilt — the exposé runs without your side of it.`,
                    coach_r4_facedown:      `<strong>That faces it down honestly.</strong> An assurable disclosure plus leading with evidence — the abatement spend, the assured baseline, the rail's modelled emissions, and an honest concession on what's still offset — is a story that survives the covenant test, the regulator and my piece.`,

